Harmattan-Loom
Ingredients: AFROBEAT + TRIP HOP + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Synth Strings + 1980s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The call-and-response of container ships greeting each other across foggy harbor roadsteads.

Harmattan-Loom is a slow-motion collision of West African polyrhythm and Bristol downbeat gloom, blanketed under vast, weightless canopies of 1980s analog synth strings. Interlocking Afrobeat percussion and muted, cyclical guitar grooves are dragged down to a sluggish, narcotic crawl, leaving cavernous spaces where massive low-end brass and filtered synthesizer swells answer one another like ocean freighters calling through blind harbor fog. Over these deep, sub-heavy nautical currents float gossamer, breathy vocal improvisations bathed in tape-delayed reverbs, evoking the eerie warmth of coastal fogbanks meeting equatorial dust.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 74–84 BPM. Loose, half-time Afrobeat swing where intricate shekere, conga, and woodblock patterns interlock across languid, bass-heavy trip hop breakbeats.
* Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Phrygian minor modal washes, bittersweet suspended fourth chords, and nostalgic 1980s lush analog pads tuned to slow, melancholic nautical drifts.
* Production Signatures: 
    1. Roadstead Horn Exchanges: Dual baritone saxophones and brassy analog synthesizer swells tracked hard left and right, trading long, low-frequency drones soaked in long spring delays to simulate maritime foghorns.
    2. Tape-Gauze Saturation: 1980s analog synth strings (Solina and Roland Juno) sampled down to 12-bit textures and re-amplified through vintage tape echo units to generate a thick, misty high-end haze.
    3. Dynamic Console Muting: Dub-style mixing desk manipulation where rhythm guitars and talking drums suddenly evaporate into deep space, leaving only sustained bass notes and shimmering synth atmospheres.
* Dynamics: Expansive, tidal movements; tracks build imperceptibly from skeletal percussion and droning horn blasts into lush, blooming walls of ethereal synth strings before receding back into cavernous silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
Harmattan-Loom coalesced in the late 1980s within the damp customs houses and cold-storage docks of Liverpool and Rotterdam, pioneered by second-generation Nigerian and Ghanaian maritime workers jamming with post-punk tape-dub experimentalists. During the bleak winter months, these musicians spent long nights listening to the low-frequency drone of container ships moored in the Mersey and Maas roadsteads, using radar whistles and foghorns as unintentional metronomes. The subculture met in clandestine warehouse sound-clashes known as "Anchor Sleeps," where attendees wore oilskin deck jackets, hand-dyed indigo workwear, and thick sea-wool beanies, moving in hypnotic, slow-motion steps through dense, sweet clove-tobacco smoke and dry-ice fog. The scene’s foundational ritual was "The Sounding": at the peak of a performance, the rhythm section would fall dead silent, permitting two detuned synth patches and a lone baritone horn to trade deep, reverberant notes across the warehouse walls, mimicking giant hulls greeting in the night.

Central Instruments
* Synth Strings: Roland VP-330 Vocoder Plus, Solina String Ensemble (processed through Boss CE-2 chorus), Roland Juno-60 string presets
* Rhythm & Percussion: Shekere, talking drum (Dundun), congas, Akai S900-sampled dusty acoustic breakbeats
* Horns & Bass: Low baritone saxophone, muted valve trombone, 1980s Moog Rogue sub-bass, flatwound Fender Jazz Bass
* Guitars & Keys: Single-coil electric guitar (palm-muted, chicken-scratch patterns drenched in Roland Space Echo RE-201), Rhodes Mark II through phasers

